raise your hand if you think you've  checked your phone more than a dozen  times already this morning how about if  you think you spend too much time  checking email or otherwise glued to a  piece of technology I certainly do  too often I find myself hunched over my  laptop and vast library trying to make a  dent in a day's deluge of emails I'm  frantically slamming away at the  keyboard looking up at the clock and  frustration as my free hour of time  slips away as soon as I think I'm making  some progress I hate refresh and then  I'm right back where I started  technology speeds up our lives but it  comes at a cost we spend so much time  reacting getting pulled in a million  different directions at once makes it  really hard to think deliberately to  live intentionally makes it hard to  manage stress foster creativity but I  have good news there's a solution it's  the oldest trick in the book  step outside and get in touch with  nature you just might like it it was a  brisk early winter morning in my  hometown of Boulder Colorado I was just  back for the Thanksgiving break for my  first eight weeks of business school and  man was I exhausted  I've been sprinting non-stop to keep up  with everything that this new high-speed  life had been throwing at me participate  in strategy class so it looks like I  belong in this place drive to a clean  tech conference and impress those  potential employers go out to bars so I  seen fun and make some friends squeeze  every potential ounce of value out of  this opportunity but put on a smile and  act like it's easy  I was stressed out sleep-deprived I  didn't feel like myself I got out of my  car I bolder's Chautauqua park trailhead  put my phone on the seat and took off  running up the mud and snow-covered  trail I didn't know where I was going or  what I would do when I got there I just  knew I needed some space I found that  space on a rock with a viewpoint  overlooking the Flatirons so i sat there  nourishing silence began to wash over me  the crisp clear mountain air filled my  lungs a fresh breeze made the spruce  trees sway peacefully in the wind and  put a little extra lift under that Hawks  wing soaring overhead I could feel my  mind slowing down about an hour later  scribbled some thoughts down in my  journal before returning to my car be  patient be grateful and get outside I  came back to Stanford that next week and  I could feel my feet on the ground again  look my friends in the eye and be fully  present for my conversations I began to  meditate a practice that I've continued  to this day  nature can change your life and it is  right outside your doorstep the benefits  of interaction with nature have been  proven to us in numerous academic  studies they include everything from  increased pain tolerance to better  memory even reduce symptoms of ADHD  studies from top universities around the  country and the world show the time in  nature significantly reduces stress and  increases creativity but this wisdoms  been around for long before the academic  studies  many world languages have words  specifically for the concepts that  getting outside is good for the mind  body and spirit in Norwegian that word  is free loose-leaf the japanese word  jinlun yoku translates directly into  english as forest bathing many of our  world's most creative and brilliant  people seem to share the same wisdom  Albert Einstein once told us look deep  into nature and then you'll understand  everything better perhaps it's no  coincidence that both the Buddha and  Isaac Newton came to their world  altering realizations while sitting  beneath the tree I wonder what our world  might look like if we all looked deep  into nature more often it shouldn't be  that hard right you can find nature  basically everywhere well the sad part  is based on the latest statistics you  probably won't as our culture spends  more and more time interacting with  technology we're spending less and less  time interacting with nature since the  mid 1980s the average time spent outside  by the American child has dropped in  half 25% fewer American adults  participate in outdoor recreation  meanwhile the time we spend in front of  screens is skyrocketed and the US were  now up to an average of seven and a half  hours a day New York Times bestselling  author Richard Louv has a name for this  crisis he calls it nature deficit  disorder and I think it's pretty serious  but the good news is the antidote is  abundant in fact you can find it  anywhere on this campus you just have to  go looking for it louvre calls it  vitamin n for nature  take it regularly turn your phone on  silent and go lay out on the grass for a  while take a walk around and really  observe the intricacies of the bushes  and the trees open yourself up let  nature come pouring it when I finished  my hike above Boulder last Thanksgiving  my vitamin End levels were at a pretty  good high but they slowly started coming  back down again over time by the time  last summer rolled around I was feeling  pretty down this time it was a deeper  more existential kind of low you see my  dream job summer internship turned out  to not have this elusive get me out of  bed in the morning quality that I've  been searching for I felt lost confused  alone in my search for a happy and  meaningful life so at the end of my  summer I decided to embark on a Vision  Quest a two-week introspective journey  in nature culminating with a four-day  wilderness Sola in the desert of  southern Utah that journey the mornings  I was woken up with the deserts sunlight  on my face the towering sand stone  monoliths that stood above me and seemed  to wait for me to ask just the right  question the deafening violence of the  thunderstorm the deafening silence of  the watercolor sunset  that journey may not fit fully into  words but what I can put into words is  the impact that it's had on me I now  know what it feels like to listen fully  to my own inner compass I've overcome  numerous mental and emotional barriers  that have stifled me my entire life I  found a deeper vision for my future for  how I hope to give my life to this world  that journey in the desert is the reason  that I'm standing here giving this talk  today so what's your next step in your  own personal journey in nature it  doesn't have to be a 4-day wilderness  solo but I do hope it pushes you just a  little bit beyond what you're used to  you might have to invest a little extra  time and effort upfront to get out there  well I assure you the return on your  investment will be worth it you'll come  back clear-headed more productive and  less stressed and what's more you will  fuel your soul for me nature is the  ultimate freedom the natural world  doesn't judge me it welcomes me for  exactly who I am nature brings me  clarity humility and infinite  possibility a starlit sky reminds me  that I am so small but that I'm a part  of something so big an incredible nature  makes me fully alive and present in  every moment a mountain top view brings  me out of my thoughts and envelops me in  the wonder of the miracle of life the RO  once said I took a walk in the woods  and I came out taller than the trees  I wonder what some time in nature might  mean for you
 

